soc: sunxi: h713-arisc: what the device says about the EDID mask (dev20)

Comment only; no code, no behaviour, no build change.

0091c argued the order of SetEDIDVersion from the firmware's own dispatcher
(AP3l section 1.4): the handler masks the byte to four bits, keeps it at
g_Data + 4 and republishes the ports whose bit changed, so the command sent
before fragment 0 would publish the firmware's ROM default and the command
sent behind fragment 7 is idempotent. The order was right; one sentence of
the reasoning was not.

The HY310 run of 22.09.2026 (debug kernel, umbau/test-20260915/dev20-20260922.md,
"Q5 mask semantics") exercised it. `edid-version 1` alone: the firmware holds
0x01, and port 0 still serves block 0 - the PC reads byte 130 = 0x4c, also
after `hpd 0 reset`. `edid-version 1` followed by the full `edid` sequence:
port 0 publishes block 1, the PC reads byte 130 = 0x54 and offers 1080p120.
Mask 0 plus the sequence brings block 0 back. So SetEDIDVersion does not
republish anything by itself; the mask is applied where fragment 7 publishes.

That makes 0091c's order load-bearing rather than tidy. The default 0 is
unaffected - fragment 7 publishes with it, and the review's guard sends the
command at all only when the firmware's byte differs - but a mask *change*
needs the upload behind it, which on a bench is `edid-version N` and then
`edid` on the debugfs cmd file. Both comment blocks now say so.

One oddity goes in with it: after the sequence with mask 1 the byte read back
as 0x0f, all four bits, where 0x01 was asked for; with 0 it read 0x00. The
status file reports it as "held want", so the divergence is visible rather
than silent. Whether the handler ORs the bits or the publish path writes it
is open - it changes nothing for the default, and the byte is a readback, not
an input.

Tested on the HY310: no code changed, so nothing to test; the comments were
written from that device run.

diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
@@ -1688,6 +1688,13 @@
  * not a description. The handler masks it to four bits, stores it at
  * g_Data + 4 and republishes only the ports whose bit changed; an unchanged
  * value returns at 0x125f0 having published nothing (AP3l 1).
+ *
+ * The device contradicts that republish. With the mask moved to 1 and
+ * nothing else done, port 0 kept block 0 -- byte 130 still 0x4c at the PC,
+ * across a hot-plug reset as well. So this command stores the byte and
+ * nothing more; the store is served with it at the next publish. Read back
+ * after a masked upload the byte was 0x0f where 0x01 was asked for, which
+ * the status file shows as "held want" (dev20, 22.09.2026).
  */
 static int arisc_edid_mask_locked(struct h713_arisc *a, u8 mask)
 {
@@ -1769,6 +1776,11 @@
 	 * ResetEDIDModule the byte is 0 - the default mask - on every probe.
 	 * The byte is readable, so a mask the firmware already holds is not
 	 * sent at all (Q5 review, O2).
+	 *
+	 * The device makes this order load-bearing rather than merely tidy: a
+	 * mask change takes effect only with the upload behind it, so changing
+	 * one at a bench means "edid-version N" and then "edid" on cmd. The
+	 * guarded default 0 needs no second step (dev20, 22.09.2026).
 	 */
 	if (readb(sram_at(a, a->off.edid_version)) != a->edid_mask) {
 		ret = arisc_edid_mask_locked(a, a->edid_mask);
